== How May Faith Be Illustrated?

To make the matter of faith clearer still, I will give you a few illustrations. Though the Holy Spirit alone can make my reader see, it is my duty and my joy to give all the light I can, and to pray the divine Lord to open blind eyes. Oh, that my reader would pray the same prayer for himself!

The faith that saves has its parallels in the human body.

It is #emph[the eye] that looks. By the eye we bring into the mind what is far away; we can bring the sun and the distant stars into the mind with a glance of the eye. So by trust we bring the Lord Jesus near to us; and though he is far away in heaven, he enters our heart. Only look to Jesus; for the hymn is strictly true—

#quote[“There is life in a look at the Crucified One, \ There is life at this moment for thee.”]

Faith is #emph[the hand] that grasps. When our hand takes hold of anything for itself, it does exactly what faith does when it takes Christ and the blessings of his redemption as its own. Faith says, “Jesus is mine.” Faith hears of the pardoning blood, and cries, “I accept it to pardon #emph[me].” Faith calls the legacies of the dying Jesus her own; and they are her own, for faith is Christ’s heir; he has given himself and all that he has to faith. Take, my friend, what grace has provided for you. You will not be a thief, for you have divine permission: “Whoever desires, let him take the water of life freely.” The man who may have a treasure simply by grasping it will be foolish indeed if he remains poor.

Faith is #emph[the mouth] that feeds on Christ. Before food can nourish us, it must be taken into us. This is a simple matter — this eating and drinking. We willingly take our food into the mouth, and then we consent to its passing down into our inner parts, where it is taken up and absorbed into our body. Paul says, in his Epistle to the Romans, in the tenth chapter, “The word is near you; it is in your mouth.” Now then, all that remains to be done is to swallow it, to let it go down into the soul. Oh, that men had an appetite! For the man who is hungry and sees food before him does not need to be taught how to eat. “Give me,” said one, “a knife and a fork and a chance.” He was quite ready to do the rest. Truly, a heart that hungers and thirsts after Christ has only to know that he is freely given, and it will receive him at once. If my reader is in that state, let him receive Jesus without hesitating; for he may be sure that he will never be blamed for doing so: “for to all who did receive Him, He gave the right to become sons of God.” He never turns anyone away, but he gives all who come the right to remain sons forever.

The occupations of daily life illustrate faith in many ways. The farmer buries good seed in the earth, and expects it not only to live but to be multiplied. He has faith in God’s covenant that seedtime and harvest shall not cease, and he is rewarded for his faith.

The merchant places his money in the care of a banker, and trusts entirely to the honesty and soundness of the bank. He entrusts his capital to another’s hands, and feels far more at ease than if he had the solid gold locked up in an iron safe.

The sailor trusts himself to the sea. When he swims he takes his foot off the bottom and rests on the buoyant ocean. He could not swim if he did not throw himself wholly on the water.

The goldsmith puts precious metal into the fire, which seems eager to consume it, but he gets it back from the furnace purified by the heat.

You cannot turn anywhere in life without seeing faith at work between man and man, or between man and the laws of nature. Now, just as we trust in daily life, so we are to trust in God as he reveals himself in Christ Jesus.

Faith exists in different people in different degrees, according to the amount of their knowledge or their growth in grace. Sometimes faith is little more than a simple #emph[clinging] to Christ: a sense of dependence and a willingness to depend in that way. When you are down at the seaside you will see limpets sticking to the rock. You walk softly up to the rock; you strike the limpet a quick blow with your walking stick, and off he comes. Try the next limpet the same way. You have given him warning; he heard the blow with which you struck his neighbor, and he clings with all his might. You will never get him off — not you! Strike, and strike again, but you will break the rock first. Our little friend, the limpet, does not know much, but he clings. He knows nothing of the geology of the rock, but he clings. He can cling, and he has found something to cling to: this is his whole stock of knowledge, and he uses it for his security and salvation. It is the limpet’s life to cling to the rock, and it is the sinner’s life to cling to Jesus. Thousands of God’s people have no more faith than this; they know enough to cling to Jesus with all their heart and soul, and this is enough for present peace and eternal safety. Jesus Christ is to them a Savior strong and mighty, a rock that cannot be moved or changed; they hold on to him for dear life, and this clinging saves them. Reader, can you not cling? Do so at once.

Faith is seen when one man relies on another because he knows the other is greater than himself. This is a higher faith: the faith that knows the reason for its dependence, and acts on it. I do not think the limpet knows much about the rock; but as faith grows it becomes more and more intelligent. A blind man trusts himself to his guide because he knows that his friend can see, and, trusting, he walks where his guide leads him. If the poor man was born blind he does not know what sight is; but he knows that there is such a thing as sight, and that his friend has it, and so he freely puts his hand into the hand of the one who sees, and follows his lead. “We walk by faith, not by sight.” “Blessed are those who have not seen and yet have believed.” This is as good a picture of faith as there could be; we know that Jesus has merit, and power, and blessing, which we do not have, and so we gladly trust ourselves to him, to be to us what we cannot be to ourselves. We trust him as the blind man trusts his guide. He never betrays our confidence; but he “has become for us wisdom from God: our righteousness, sanctification, and redemption.”

Every boy who goes to school has to exercise faith while he learns. His teacher teaches him geography, and tells him about the shape of the earth and the existence of certain great cities and empires. The boy does not himself know that these things are true, except that he believes his teacher and the books put into his hands. That is what you will have to do with Christ, if you are to be saved: you must simply know because he tells you, believe because he assures you it is so, and trust yourself to him because he promises you that salvation will be the result. Almost all that you and I know has come to us by faith. A scientific discovery has been made, and we are sure of it. On what grounds do we believe it? On the authority of certain well-known men of learning, whose reputations are established. We have never made or seen their experiments, but we believe their testimony. You must do the same with Jesus: because he teaches you certain truths, you are to be his disciple and believe his words; because he has performed certain acts, you are to be his client and trust yourself to him. He is infinitely greater than you, and offers himself to your trust as your Master and Lord. If you will receive him and his words, you will be saved.

Another and higher form of faith is the faith that #emph[grows out of love]. Why does a boy trust his father? The reason the child trusts his father is that he loves him. Blessed and happy are those who have a sweet faith in Jesus, intertwined with deep affection for him, for this is a restful confidence. These lovers of Jesus are charmed by his character and delighted with his mission; they are carried away by the loving-kindness that he has shown, and so they cannot help trusting him, because they admire, revere, and love him so much.

Loving trust in the Savior may be illustrated like this. A lady is the wife of the most eminent physician of the day. She is taken with a dangerous illness, and is struck down by its power; yet she is wonderfully calm and quiet, for her husband has made this disease his special study, and has healed thousands who suffered from it. She is not in the least troubled, for she feels perfectly safe in the hands of one so dear to her, in whom skill and love are blended in their highest forms. Her faith is reasonable and natural; her husband, from every point of view, deserves it from her. This is the kind of faith which the happiest of believers place in Christ. There is no physician like him; none can save as he can. We love him, and he loves us, and so we put ourselves into his hands, accept whatever he prescribes, and do whatever he tells us. We feel that nothing can be wrongly arranged while he is directing our affairs; for he loves us too well to let us perish, or suffer a single needless pang.

Faith is the root of obedience, and this may be clearly seen in everyday life. When a captain trusts a pilot to steer his ship into port, he handles the ship as the pilot directs. When a traveler trusts a guide to lead him over a difficult pass, he follows the path his guide points out. When a patient believes in a physician, he carefully follows his prescriptions and directions. Faith that refuses to obey the commands of the Savior is a mere pretense, and will never save the soul. We trust Jesus to save us; he gives us directions about the way of salvation; we follow those directions and are saved. Let my reader not forget this. Trust Jesus, and prove your trust by doing whatever he tells you.

A notable form of #emph[faith arises from sure knowledge]\; this comes from growth in grace, and is the faith that believes Christ because it knows him, and trusts him because it has tested him and found him unfailingly faithful. An old Christian used to write T and P in the margin of her Bible whenever she had tried and proved a promise. How easy it is to trust a tried and proved Savior! You cannot do this yet, but you will. Everything must have a beginning. You will rise to strong faith in due time. This mature faith does not ask for signs and tokens, but bravely believes. Look at the faith of the master mariner — I have often marveled at it. He casts off his cable; he steams away from the land. For days, weeks, or even months, he sees neither sail nor shore; yet on he goes, day and night, without fear, until one morning he finds himself exactly opposite the desired haven he has been steering for. How has he found his way across the trackless deep? He has trusted in his compass, his nautical almanac, his glass, and the heavenly bodies; and by following their guidance, without sighting land, he has steered so accurately that he does not have to change course by a single point to enter port. It is a wonderful thing — that sailing or steaming without sight. Spiritually it is a blessed thing to leave the shores of sight and feeling altogether, and to say “Goodbye” to inward feelings, cheering providences, signs, tokens, and so on. It is glorious to be far out on the ocean of divine love, believing in God, and steering straight for heaven by the directions of the Word of God. “Blessed are those who have not seen and yet have believed”; an abundant entrance will be administered to them at the last, and a safe voyage on the way. Will my reader not put his trust in God in Christ Jesus? There I rest with joyful confidence. Brother, come with me, and believe our Father and our Savior. Come at once.
